00:00:06.052 --> 00:00:08.260
STEPHANIE WONG: Welcome back
to the "Next Live Show,"

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.490
I'm Stephanie Wong.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.265
Joining us now is Jennifer Lind,
Product Management Director.

00:00:12.265 --> 00:00:13.640
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:14.660
JENNIFER LIN: Thanks
for having me.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:15.820
STEPHANIE WONG: So for
those viewers who are just

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.507
learning about
Kubernetes and Borg,

00:00:17.507 --> 00:00:19.090
can you explain what
each of those is,

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.460
and how we're making
Kubernetes easier to consume

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.238
and the significance of how
each has evolved at Google?

00:00:24.238 --> 00:00:25.030
JENNIFER LIN: Sure.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.740
Kubernetes is the
externalization

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.440
and the open source of our
internal controller development

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.510
container orchestration
tool called Borg.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.270
It's something that we've
evolved over the last decade,

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.758
and we have said
publicly that we

00:00:35.758 --> 00:00:37.300
launch four billion
containers a week

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.740
across the Google environment.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.020
So managing that at
scale, we've learned a lot

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.240
about just running large
global scale systems

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.370
and doing container
orchestration at scale

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.690
in a resilient, reliable way.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.780
Kubernetes was open
sourced a few years ago,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.450
and we've had our own
managed version of Kubernetes

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.830
with Google Kubernetes
Engine now over four years.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.340
So a lot has happened in a
very short period of time,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.590
but there's lots of lessons
learned under the covers.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.960
STEPHANIE WONG: We continue
to talk about API, services

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:02.980
and open source.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.530
Why is Google differentiating
in this space?

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.290
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah, I think this
industry is changing so fast,

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.780
developers writing new software,
so it's really important

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.460
that we get the
interfaces and how

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.410
to enable the lifecycle
management of services

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.970
right as a system.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.180
And I think that's why
Kubernetes has also gained

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.750
a lot of traction very quickly.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.890
It sort of makes the notion of
a service a first class citizen.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.500
And we've been very clear
about how do APIs interact,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.237
and how do we essentially
define services

00:01:28.237 --> 00:01:30.070
and do the lifecycle
management of services.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:32.112
And that's all coming with
the maturity of things

00:01:32.112 --> 00:01:33.307
like Kubernetes and Istio.

00:01:33.307 --> 00:01:35.890
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, and this
is all enabling our developers.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.470
A lot of developers want to
approach the way that Google

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.420
does and dive in and
get experience the way

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.290
that Google develops software.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.980
How does open source
enable the community

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.310
and empower the community?

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.050
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah,
I think open source

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.000
has been the reason why a lot
of this has moved so quickly.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.505
I mean, it feels like
overnight it has become,

00:01:52.505 --> 00:01:54.880
Kubernetes has become sort of
the container orchestration

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.212
system of choice.

00:01:56.212 --> 00:01:57.670
Just a few years
ago, there were so

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.310
many, there was a lot of
fragmentation, a lot of folks

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.530
understood the value
proposition of containerization

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.252
with Docker.

00:02:03.252 --> 00:02:05.710
But essentially managing that
at scale at the system level,

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.330
there were various options.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.150
Through open source, I think
we've allowed a lot of people

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.470
to just, number one,
understand what's going on.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.032
Number two, contribute in a
way that's meaningful for them.

00:02:15.032 --> 00:02:17.240
And get a lot of the use
cases out there in the open,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.480
so we can iterate on best
practices, et cetera.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.650
And yes,--

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.350
A lot of this has been very
focused on developer agility,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.840
but now as we move into
sort of the maturity of it,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.750
yes, a lot of the practices for
the operational administrators

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.380
and SREs and making this
essentially a production level

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.660
system, is a lot of
what the community is

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.680
excited about as well.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:36.400
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's great, yeah.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.530
Open source is really
focused on our users.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.900
And we also want to
build our user experience

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:41.710
into our products.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.460
Can you talk a little
bit more about that?

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.470
And given that our cloud
users are different

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:46.540
and they have
different needs, how

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.410
do we build that into
our products as well?

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.050
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah, I mean, on
the product side within Google,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.700
we do a lot with the
user research team

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.950
to just make sure we understand,
who are the personas,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.480
whether it's
developer or security

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.520
administrator or a
network administrator

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.268
or the IT professional
or the user.

00:03:00.268 --> 00:03:02.560
So just making sure we taking
sort of a use case driven

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.390
approach, and
understanding sort of who

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.070
is the user of the product.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.445
And essentially, what problem
are they trying to solve?

00:03:08.445 --> 00:03:09.820
And how do we hide
the complexity

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:10.870
and make it a lot easier?

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.453
And make sure that
they're essentially

00:03:12.453 --> 00:03:13.425
doing their job well.

00:03:13.425 --> 00:03:14.800
But there are a
lot, because this

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.930
is a software stack
with a lot of,

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.330
we've talked a lot about
decoupling and separation

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:20.345
of concerns.

00:03:20.345 --> 00:03:21.970
You could have a
unified stack, but you

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.512
have to recognize that there's
lots of folks that essentially

00:03:24.512 --> 00:03:26.140
are consumers of that stack.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.310
STEPHANIE WONG: So what's
your advice for those

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.310
who want to move to the
Cloud with acceleration,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.390
and not have to do
a rip and replace,

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.220
and manage their
existing lifecycle?

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.385
How do they bring
in Cloud Native?

00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:37.510
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah, I think
Cloud Native has really

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.930
been about sort of openness
and the flexibility

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.300
that we talked about today
with Anthos is really,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.150
there are an architectural
set of principles,

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.340
which I think Kubernetes
and things like Istio

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.777
have kind of put out there
in the open source community.

00:03:50.777 --> 00:03:51.610
There's a framework.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.240
And then there's
an implementation.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.330
So number one, I think
the industry is really

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.030
rallying around the fact
that container orchestration,

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:01.682
there's sort of a clear
de facto standard there.

00:04:01.682 --> 00:04:03.640
Obviously, we have our
managed version of that,

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.140
because there's a lot beyond
just the software bits in terms

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.410
of how do you actually
do lifecycle management

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:12.310
and keep essentially automatic
upgrades, patching, security

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.950
updates, et cetera, as
part of this system,

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.600
so that developers
can move quickly,

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.000
but you can keep essentially
the security and stability

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:19.630
of the system.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:19.959
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:21.880
And one big part
of the acceleration

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.462
is GKE on prem, which was
announced last August.

00:04:24.462 --> 00:04:26.170
And this is the first
time we've actually

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.940
brought our technology into
the data center at this scale.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.010
So why now?

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.320
And why not sooner?

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.020
JENNIFER LIN: I
mean, what we found

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.020
is, everybody understands
the benefits of Cloud.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.270
But in many cases, there's
technical and non-technical

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.687
reasons why they can't move
all their workloads into Cloud

00:04:38.687 --> 00:04:39.220
overnight.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.400
So this was a lot about bringing
the best of the Kubernetes

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.410
stack and what we're
doing with Google Cloud

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.690
to the on prem environment,
and helping people move

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:47.800
at their own pace.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.970
While at the same time,
modernizing in place.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.940
So it's not just about
renting compute cycles.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.190
It's actually about
application modernization

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.870
and developing new
services and adding

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:57.230
more business value, et cetera.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.810
So we're pretty
excited about that.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.180
Now it's not just on
our GCP resources,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.270
but we can essentially run
this on third party servers

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.420
in a private data center
for the enterprise.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.795
And as we talked about today,
other environments as well.

00:05:08.795 --> 00:05:09.900
STEPHANIE WONG: So
speaking of modernization,

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.810
we loved your demo during
the day one keynote.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:12.540
JENNIFER LIN: Thank you.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.310
STEPHANIE WONG: We heard a
lot about modernizing in place

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:14.950
this year.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.830
Can you talk about
how Anthos enables

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.370
you to modernize
your applications,

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.870
no matter where they are, as
you mention, on premise or user

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:21.480
in the Cloud?

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.522
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah, I
think that's the nice thing

00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:25.170
about Kubernetes
is, with Anthos,

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.930
we're really thinking
about sort of open APIs.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.720
So that the interfaces between
those environments, number one,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.310
we can hide the
complexity, but still

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.230
keep a consistent
management environment.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.690
For the developers, independent
of where those workloads

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.840
run, they can learn
one set of tools

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.300
and essentially evolve
as the industry evolves

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.410
without picking sort of exactly
where that's going to run

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.432
or what production environment
it's going to run in,

00:05:48.432 --> 00:05:49.890
because many
developers don't know.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.760
They want to write
once, run anywhere.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.150
Their consumers may be
running in many, many clouds.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.090
And similarly for the
platform administrator,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.180
they don't have to learn a
bunch of different vendor tools.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.260
This framework is here to stay.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.980
We believe it's going to be
the standard for many years

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:05.680
to come.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.910
So even if that's not running
in Google's Cloud per se,

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.740
that skill set that
many enterprises

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.560
are trying to hire people who
understand Kubernetes, that's

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:14.220
very portable.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.470
So people are willing
to invest in it now

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.133
because it's going to be
here to stay for many years.

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:20.550
STEPHANIE WONG:
And on that theme,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.550
we're hearing that
developers and operators want

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.720
to be managing at higher
levels of the stack.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.450
But they still want visibility
and control over policy

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.310
management at a service level.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.860
How is Anthos providing a
unified programming model

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.632
and monitoring and policy across
on premise and multiple clouds?

00:06:34.632 --> 00:06:36.090
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah,
great question.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.620
I mean, with
Kubernetes obviously,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.960
we've really thought about
container orchestration

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.480
and cluster
administration at scale.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.758
With Istio, we're
thinking about assuming

00:06:44.758 --> 00:06:46.800
most of your microservices
are now containerized,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.960
how do you look out for
the lifecycle and health

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.543
of those services
and the interactions

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:51.570
between those services?

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.987
How do you make sure that those
services are authenticated

00:06:53.987 --> 00:06:57.780
and that you can put essentially
policies around those services

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.360
without having to manage the
underlying infrastructure

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.193
complexity?

00:07:01.193 --> 00:07:02.610
And then we showed
how we're doing

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.820
config management and automating
essentially configurations

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.400
at scale in a way that
essentially is declarative.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.890
So you can define the policies
once and push them down

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.000
to different
environments, and you

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.875
don't have to rewrite
them with a lot of toil

00:07:13.875 --> 00:07:15.060
for each cloud environment.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.980
And that's really
important for customers

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.440
that are thinking about,
let's say, PCI compliance

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.740
or governance.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.040
Those rules around how
to have those security

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.262
controls in place don't
change cloud to cloud.

00:07:25.262 --> 00:07:26.970
But today, they're
spending a lot of time

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.140
just to make sure they can
ensure audit and compliance

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.390
in each different environment.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:33.300
So we believe that's
sort of extra overhead

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.925
that doesn't need to
be there, and that

00:07:34.925 --> 00:07:36.690
was a key push with Anthos.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.430
Many of our
enterprise customers,

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:41.010
they want to embrace
new technologies,

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:42.600
but it's not easy
to figure out, how

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.010
do I ensure that I'm
still reducing my costs,

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:46.820
keeping the efficiency
and addressing

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.320
what the auditors and compliance
regulatory environments are

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:49.883
looking for?

00:07:49.883 --> 00:07:50.800
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.860
And we actually know
that not everybody

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.480
has the luxury of
moving to the cloud

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.400
immediately, for
whatever reason.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.140
How can we help them get there?

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.430
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah, I think
a lot of it is, I mean,

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:00.990
this has been a
great week so far.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:03.750
I think the partner
community is very engaged.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.390
I think a lot of the use cases
and the best practices that

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:09.390
are being put out there
are based on folks

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.600
trying things out,
sharing best practices.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.500
We're trying to take more of
a use case driven approach.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.090
With Anthos, I think a lot
of what we're trying to share

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.620
is the operational
domain knowledge,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.320
and the best practices of
us having run these systems

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.230
at scale.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:26.790
But also tapping into our
hundreds of ecosystem partners

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:29.310
who are getting excited about
it and contributing back.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.230
As this matures,
and I think this

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.590
is happening very quickly
in the last couple of years,

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.780
but it really is about
solution delivery and not

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.197
just technology delivery.

00:08:38.197 --> 00:08:39.780
STEPHANIE WONG: With
Anthos, all these

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.613
customers moving into
production [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:41.613 --> 00:08:43.117
skills, very exciting.

00:08:43.117 --> 00:08:45.450
And obviously, as you said,
there are security concerns.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.037
How are we making that
easier for our customers?

00:08:48.037 --> 00:08:49.620
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah,
I mean we've been,

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.020
security is a major
differentiator for us for GCP

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:53.040
more broadly.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:54.540
And I think because
containerization

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.930
is relatively a new space,
we've been doing a lot with both

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:01.080
our customers and partners
to do number one, education

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.120
and awareness round
container security

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.860
and secure software
supply chains

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.323
and things like binary
authorization and CICD,

00:09:07.323 --> 00:09:09.240
and how to make sure
that security is baked in

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.200
way upstream,--

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.030
So that when a developer
checks in code,

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.570
essentially it starts the
governance process of security.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.760
And it's not a bolt on
afterthought after the fact.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.480
I think that is a lot of the way
that the Google developers are.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.980
When they check in
code, essentially they

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.605
don't have to be an
expert in security,

00:09:24.605 --> 00:09:27.060
but the platform takes care
of a lot of the complexity

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.740
of security and governance.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.120
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you so
much for your time, Jennifer.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.578
JENNIFER LIN: Thanks
for having me.

